# Deep tablebase-conversion positions — see test/tb_insearch.py (`--deep`).
#
# Every line is `<FEN> ; <material> <oracle verdict at the stated clock>`, and every
# one is a 6- or 7-MAN position that White WINS. This set exists because the ≤5-man
# suite (test/tb_positions.epd) cannot reach the in-search WDL probe at all any more:
# a ≤5-man root is DTZ-ranked, which zeroes C.tbCardinality and switches the in-search
# probe OFF for the whole search (src/search.cpp, start()). The probe therefore only
# runs under a root the ROOT probe could not rank — in practice a root with MORE men
# than the tables hold, whose search descends into ≤5-man nodes. That is also the
# common case in a real game: the board is 6+ men long before it is 5.
#
# ORACLE. Syzygy cannot judge these roots (the tables are 5-man), so each line was
# verified with Stockfish 18 (/Users/tim.alexander/sf18-arm/src/stockfish, 6 threads,
# 8s, AT THE STATED HALFMOVE CLOCK — SF's search is clock-aware, so a win the counter
# had already eaten would show as ~0). Only positions where SF returned a MATE score
# or a tablebase-band score are here; nothing was kept on a "looks winning" cp value.
# SF was run WITHOUT SyzygyPath: SF18 SIGBUSes on this machine whenever it probes our
# table files, which is an SF/box problem and not ours — at depth 46-133 with no
# tables it is still a far stronger oracle than the position needs.
#
# HIGH CLOCK. Half the set is the same position with a high halfmove clock, chosen so
# SF still returns a mate at that clock. Those are the lines that matter for the
# in-search probe's rule50 gate: without it, a search descending into ≤5-man nodes
# reads "won with a fresh clock" for a clock that is nearly spent.
#
# Re-verify:  gawk -F' ;' '!/^#/&&NF{print $1}' test/tb_positions_deep.epd | <sf-oracle>

# ---- 6-man ----
